e was  the island needed to be destroyed  and you needed to also  create a  story that would end in  you know planting the seed for the  proliferation of these dinosaurs that  would be the landscape that would set up  the third movie so it was it was very  much an interesting challenge for the  writers and for j.a  to deliver a movie that was structurally  really kind of unlike any of the  jurassic films that had come before and  so because of that movie we're able to  do what we're now doing in this film  which is the big payoff the big uh  question of can humans and dinosaurs  coexist not during a mishap in a theme  park in this sort of like man over  animal in a zoo type of scenario but  rather  in a world where the  pandora's box has been opened this  technology exists these animals exist  all around the world what does that look  like and can man and creature coexist  well you know fallen kingdom was  directed by j.a bayona who's a brilliant  filmmaker and i had the opportunity to  watch another director work from start  to finish and make a film and i i  learned so much from him i honestly  think i'm a better director as a result  of getting to watch him work and the  film we made there told a story that we  pay off in in uh jurassic world dominion  i i hope that once you see this movie  when you watch the whole trilogy it'll  be very clear that you know we were  telling a story the whole time  don't move  bigger why do they always have to go  bigger  it was bananas to get to be in a movie  with not just laura and jeff goldblum  and sam neil but the characters they  played in the original jurassic park  this was always designed to be the  finale for not just jurassic world but  also jurassic park so there are  storylines that were established in 1993  in the original jurassic park that get  paid off in jurassic world dominion  and to get to be in the movie that does  that  is so thrilling when we were on set  there were many times when we were  like it's true 